---
.title = "Traverse",
.description = "Walk and transform a tree with typed visitor hooks, in four modes from plain visits to full semantics.",
.author = "Arshad Yaseen",
.date = @date("2026-06-23T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---
The traverser walks a tree and calls your visitor's hooks at every node. Pick the mode for what your tool needs to know as it walks.

| Mode                      | Tracks                          | Returns                                   | For                                         |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| [`basic`](#visitors)      | the path from the root          | nothing                                   | pattern matching, counters, simple lints    |
| [`scoped`](#scoped)       | the path and lexical scopes     | a `ScopeTree`                             | checks that depend on scope or strict mode  |
| [`semantic`](#semantic)   | scopes, symbols, and references | the [`Semantic`](/parser/semantic/) model | analysis that needs to know what names mean |
| [`transform`](#transform) | the path, with a mutable tree   | nothing                                   | rewrites, codemods, and desugaring          |

The read-only modes see a `*const Tree`, so an analysis cannot mutate what it reads. Only `transform` gets a `*Tree`.

In JavaScript, [`yuku-ast`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-ast) walks any ESTree AST, and [`yuku-analyzer`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-analyzer) adds the semantic model to the walk.

## [Visitors]($section.id('visitors'))

A visitor is a struct with `enter_<tag>` and `exit_<tag>` methods, named after node tags. Each hook receives its node's payload already unpacked. This one counts functions in `basic` mode.

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const parser = @import("parser");
const ast = parser.ast;
const traverser = parser.traverser;
const basic = traverser.basic;

const Counter = struct {
    functions: u32 = 0,

    pub fn enter_function(
        self: *Counter,
        _: ast.Function,
        _: ast.NodeIndex,
        _: *basic.Ctx,
    ) traverser.Action {
        self.functions += 1;
        return .proceed;
    }
};

var counter = Counter{};
try basic.traverse(Counter, &tree, &counter);
std.debug.print("{d} functions\n", .{counter.functions});
```

A misspelled hook or a wrong payload type is a compile error. `enter_node` and `exit_node` fire for every node. The walk is depth-first with siblings in source order, and an enter hook returns `.proceed` to walk into the children, `.skip` to pass over them, or `.stop` to end the walk.

Every mode's context has `ctx.tree` and `ctx.path`, which knows the current node's `parent()`, its `ancestors()`, and its `depth()`.

## [Scoped]($section.id('scoped'))

`ctx.scope` follows the walk into and out of every scope, with its kind, `hoistTarget()`, and `isStrict()`. This lint finds each `var` that escapes the block it is written in.

```zig
const scoped = traverser.scoped;

const VarInBlock = struct {
    found: u32 = 0,

    pub fn enter_variable_declaration(
        self: *VarInBlock,
        decl: ast.VariableDeclaration,
        _: ast.NodeIndex,
        ctx: *scoped.Ctx,
    ) traverser.Action {
        if (decl.kind == .@"var" and ctx.scope.currentScope().kind == .block) self.found += 1;
        return .proceed;
    }
};

var lint = VarInBlock{};
const scopes = try scoped.traverse(VarInBlock, &tree, &lint);
```

On `{ var b; } for (var i = 0;;) {}` it finds `b` and `i`. The walk also returns every scope it saw as a `ScopeTree`, and an empty visitor returns just that.

## [Semantic]($section.id('semantic'))

The walk binds symbols and references as it goes, with `ctx.symbols` knowing every name declared so far and `ctx.inTypePosition()` telling when it is inside a TypeScript type. This visitor counts the names a file uses as types.

```zig
const sem = traverser.semantic;

const TypeNames = struct {
    count: u32 = 0,

    pub fn enter_identifier_reference(
        self: *TypeNames,
        _: ast.IdentifierReference,
        _: ast.NodeIndex,
        ctx: *sem.Ctx,
    ) traverser.Action {
        if (ctx.inTypePosition()) self.count += 1;
        return .proceed;
    }
};

var names = TypeNames{};
const semantic = try sem.traverse(TypeNames, &tree, &names);
```

The walk returns the complete [`Semantic`](/parser/semantic/) model, every reference resolved, the same one `semantic.analyze` builds. `analyze` is this mode with a visitor that reports the early errors.

## [Transform]($section.id('transform'))

A `transform` hook rewrites the tree in place. The walker re-reads a node after its enter hook, so it walks the replacement. This one turns `total = price + tax` into `total = price * tax`.

```zig
const transform = traverser.transform;

const AddToMultiply = struct {
    pub fn enter_binary_expression(
        _: *AddToMultiply,
        expr: ast.BinaryExpression,
        index: ast.NodeIndex,
        ctx: *transform.Ctx,
    ) traverser.Action {
        if (expr.operator == .add) {
            ctx.tree.setData(index, .{ .binary_expression = .{
                .left = expr.left,
                .right = expr.right,
                .operator = .multiply,
            } });
        }
        return .proceed;
    }
};

var rewrite = AddToMultiply{};
try transform.traverse(AddToMultiply, &tree, &rewrite);
```

`addNode`, `addString`, and `setIdentifierName` build and rename nodes during the walk, in the tree's arena. After wrapping a node in a new parent, return `.skip`, so the walker does not enter the moved node again. [Codegen](/parser/codegen/) prints the result.

`ast.Tree.initEmpty` starts a tree from nothing, so a transpiler can walk an input tree with full semantics while building a separate output tree. Modes compose as passes, such as a transform followed by a semantic walk over its result.

## [Your own mode]($section.id('your-own-mode'))

The four modes are four context types over one walker, each [about thirty lines](https://github.com/yuku-toolchain/yuku/blob/main/src/parser/traverser/basic.zig), and `traverser.walk` drives a context of your own the same way.
